// switch-value-x.lmc - read the three switches as a binary number and output its value each time it changes
// (extended LMC with the peripherals, NOT part of OCR's LMC)
// switch3 counts 4, switch2 counts 2 and switch1 counts 1, so 3 bits give 0 to 7.
// value = 4 x switch3 + 2 x switch2 + switch1, worked out with additions because the LMC cannot multiply.
// Run it at Medium or Fast (not Instant), then click the switches. Press Pause to stop it.
// Change one switch at a time. The program reads the three switches one after another, so if two changed while it
// was halfway through reading them it could report a value in between. Real hardware has the same problem.
loop    LDA switch3
        ADD switch3     // 2 x switch3
        ADD switch2
        STA t           // t = 2 x switch3 + switch2
        ADD t           // 4 x switch3 + 2 x switch2
        ADD switch1
        STA value
        SUB last
        BRZ loop        // the same as last time: nothing to report
        LDA value
        STA last
        OUT             // a new value: output it
        BRA loop
t       DAT
value   DAT
last    DAT 999         // 999 is never a real value, so the first pass always outputs
